module bindings.javascript.rewrite_destination_unrestricted_capture;

// A declarative rewrite rule answers one question per matched request: where
// does this request go next? The config-driven routers -- Next.js `rewrites`
// and `redirects` are the shape's largest deployment -- express that as an
// object holding a matcher and a target. The matcher is `source` plus
// `has`/`missing` entries whose `type` is one of the four documented request
// sources (query, host, header, cookie) and whose `value` is a regex whose
// named captures become parameters. The target is `destination`, and the
// request's captured values land in it as `:name`. A destination carrying a
// scheme is absolute, and a rewrite to an absolute destination is a
// server-side forward: the server issues the request and hands the response
// back to whoever matched.
//
// The matcher regex is the only thing standing between the requester and the
// bytes of that outbound target. A capture written `(?<id>.*)` accepts every
// character -- dots, slashes, at-signs, percent-encodings -- so the value the
// requester supplied becomes the authority and the path of the URL the server
// fetches, and the rule is an open proxy: it reaches internal targets the
// requester cannot and reflects the response. Writing the capture over the
// identifier's own alphabet is the control, and it is a property of the
// pattern: `(?<id>[a-f0-9]*)`, `(?<n>\d*)`, an anchored form, or a bounded
// alternative all stop the value from moving the target.
//
// The binding reads three literal facts about the rule and judges none of
// them: a documented matcher type, a capture that accepts any character, and
// a URL with a scheme. Restricting the capture removes the any-character
// literal and stops the report, whichever alphabet the author chooses. A rule
// whose destination carries no scheme stays inside the origin, so the
// requester cannot move the target off the host, and is not reported; a
// matcher with no capture at all feeds the destination nothing. All three
// clauses read string-literal tokens, which the frontend derives from the
// AST, so a comment describing the rule cannot satisfy them. The clauses sit
// at function scope, so a rule object split across a shared helper still
// reports on the helper that assembles it.
binding rewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.literal in ["query", "host", "header", "cookie"]
    and node.context.literal contains "://"
    and node.context.literal contains ">.*"
  emit issue code.RewriteTargetFromUnrestrictedCapture at node
}
